## Variables

a name that points to a value stored in memory, so you can reuse it without retyping the value every time
created just by assigning something to a name, no need to declare a type first
example: age = 20
name = "Ben"
is_student = True
naming rules: can't start with a number, can't contain spaces, case-sensitive (age and Age are different variables), can't be a reserved word like for or if
convention in python is snake_case for variable names, not camelCase

## Data types

the different kinds of values python works with, each behaving differently
the core ones: x = 5 int
y = 3.14 float
name = "Ben" str
is_on = True bool
check what type something is with type(): type(5) result <class 'int'>
type("hi") result <class 'str'>
knowing the type matters because operations behave differently depending on it — plus adds numbers but joins strings together

## Type conversion

switching a value from one type to another, done with functions named after the target type
example: int("5") result 5, string to int
str(5) result "5", int to string
float("3.14") result 3.14
int(3.9) result 3, chops off the decimal, doesn't round
comes up constantly with user input, since input() always returns a string, even if someone types a number: age = int(input("Enter your age: "))
converting something that isn't actually valid raises an error: int("hello") raises ValueError

## Operators

symbols that perform an operation on values
arithmetic: 5 + 2 result 7
5 - 2 result 3
5 * 2 result 10
5 / 2 result 2.5, always returns a float
5 // 2 result 2, floor division, drops the remainder
5 % 2 result 1, modulus, gives the remainder
5 ** 2 result 25, exponent
comparison, always returns a boolean: 5 == 2 result False
5 != 2 result True
5 > 2 result True
assignment shortcuts: x = 5
x += 1 same as x = x + 1
x -= 1
x *= 2

## Input/output

print() sends output to the screen
input() pauses the program and waits for the user to type something, then returns it as a string
example: name = input("What's your name? ")
print("Hello, " + name)
print() can take multiple values separated by commas, and adds spaces between them automatically: print("Age:", 20) result Age: 20

## Comments

lines in the code that python ignores completely, meant for humans reading the code
single line starts with a hash symbol:
this calculates the total
total = price * quantity
multi-line comments aren't a real thing in python, but a triple-quoted string not assigned to anything is often used the same way
good comments explain why something's done a certain way, not just what the line does — the code already shows what it does

## Basic syntax

python doesn't use curly braces or semicolons to mark blocks of code — it uses indentation
everything inside a block (an if statement, a loop, a function) has to be indented consistently, usually 4 spaces
example: if True:
print("this is inside the block")
print("this is not")
mixing tabs and spaces, or getting the indentation level wrong, causes an IndentationError — one of the most common early mistakes
a colon marks the start of a block, always followed by an indented line underneath

## Strings

text, wrapped in either single or double quotes — python doesn't care which, as long as it's consistent on each one a = 'hello'
b = "hello"
combine strings with plus: "hello" + " " + "world" result "hello world"
useful built-in methods: "hello".upper() result "HELLO"
"HELLO".lower() result "hello"
" hi ".strip() result "hi", removes surrounding whitespace
"a,b,c".split(",") result ["a", "b", "c"]
",".join(["a", "b"]) result "a,b"
strings are immutable, so all these methods return a new string rather than changing the original

## String formatting

building a string that has variables plugged into it
the modern way is an f-string, put an f right before the opening quote: name = "Ben"
age = 20
print(f"{name} is {age} years old")
result Ben is 20 years old
can do more than just insert values — calculations work inside the braces too: print(f"Next year you'll be {age + 1}")
older methods you'll still see in other people's code: "{} is {}".format(name, age) the .format() method
"%s is %s" % (name, age) percent formatting, older style

## Boolean logic

True and False, used to control the flow of a program
combined with logical operators: True and False result False, both need to be true
True or False result True, only one needs to be true
not True result False, flips it
values other than actual booleans get treated as "truthy" or "falsy" when checked in a condition — 0, empty string, empty list, and None all count as falsy, pretty much everything else counts as truthy if []:
print("won't print, empty list is falsy")

## if / elif / else

controls which block of code runs, based on a condition
example: age = 20 if age < 13:
print("child")
elif age < 20:
print("teenager")
else:
print("adult")
only one branch runs — python checks each condition top to bottom and stops at the first one that's true
elif and else are both optional, an if on its own is perfectly valid

## for loops

repeats a block of code once for each item in a sequence
example: for letter in "abc":
print(letter)
result a, b, c printed on separate lines
looping a set number of times uses range(): for i in range(5):
print(i)
result 0 1 2 3 4
range(start, stop, step) works the same way slicing does — start is included, stop isn't range(2, 10, 2) result 2, 4, 6, 8

## while loops

repeats a block of code for as long as a condition stays true
example: count = 0
while count < 5:
print(count)
count += 1
unlike a for loop, there's no automatic limit — forgetting to update the condition inside the loop causes an infinite loop that never stops on its own
generally reach for a for loop when you know how many times to repeat something, and a while loop when you're waiting for a condition to change

## break, continue, pass

three keywords for controlling a loop's flow from the inside
break stops the loop immediately, skips everything left, even in later iterations for i in range(10):
if i == 3:
break
print(i)
result 0 1 2
continue skips just the rest of this iteration, then moves on to the next one for i in range(5):
if i == 2:
continue
print(i)
result 0 1 3 4
